second artist, birth date for a different person, or second sport is evidence to retain it. Check each proposed deletion once more against all originals before producing the final short list.\nInput: {\"question\": \"When was the Croatian money introduced?\", \"slot_hint\": \"other\", \"all_original_sources\": {\"D1S1\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 63, \"text\": \"Croatia, they introduced the Independent State of Croatia kuna.\"}, \"D1S2\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00\", \"start\": 64, \"end\": 273, \"text\": \"This currency remained in circulation until 1945, when it – along with competing issues by the communist Partisans – disappeared with the establishment of FPR Yugoslavia and was replaced by the Yugoslav dinar.\"}, \"D1S3\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00\", \"start\": 274, \"end\": 406, \"text\": \"The modern kuna was introduced on May 30, 1994, starting a transitional period from the Croatian dinar, ending on December 31, 1994.\"}, \"D1S4\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00\", \"start\": 407, \"end\": 473, \"text\": \"The exchange rate between dinar and kuna was 1 kuna = 1000 dinars.\"}, \"D1S5\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00\", \"start\": 474, \"end\": 570, \"text\": \"Since then, Croatia has been in a system of de facto currency board with a fixed exchanged rate.\"}, \"D1S6\": {\"document_number\": 1, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00\", \"start\": 571, \"end\": 593, \"text\": \"The kuna was pegged to\"}, \"D2S1\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 152, \"text\": \"Independent State of Croatia kuna The kuna was the currency of the Independent State of Croatia in the period between 1941 and 1945 during World War II.\"}, \"D2S2\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01\", \"start\": 153, \"end\": 261, \"text\": \"The word \\\"kuna\\\" means \\\"marten\\\" in Croatian and the same word is used for the current Croatian kuna currency.\"}, \"D2S3\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01\", \"start\": 262, \"end\": 305, \"text\": \"This kuna was subdivided into 100 \\\"banica\\\".\"}, \"D2S4\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01\", \"start\": 306, \"end\": 357, \"text\": \"It was preceded and replaced by the Yugoslav dinar.\"}, \"D2S5\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01\", \"start\": 358, \"end\": 449, \"text\": \"The Croatian kuna was introduced in the Independent State of Croatia (NDH) on 26 July 1941.\"}, \"D2S6\": {\"document_number\": 2, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01\", \"start\": 450, \"end\": 573, \"text\": \"The kuna replaced the Yugoslav dinar at par and was fixed to the German Reichsmark (ℛℳ) with dual exchange rates, one fixed\"}, \"D3S1\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d02\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 20, \"text\": \"the Yugoslav dinar .\"}, \"D3S2\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d02\", \"start\": 21, \"end\": 255, \"text\": \"[ 9 ] A 5000 Independent State of Croatia kuna banknote , 1943 series From 1941–1945 during World War II in Yugoslavia , the Independent State of Croatia officially adopted the Kuna as the currency for the first time on 26 July 1941 .\"}, \"D3S3\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d02\", \"start\": 256, \"end\": 373, \"text\": \"The Independent State of Croatia kuna at the time of adoption included banknotes of 10 , 50 , 100 , 500 , and 1,000 .\"}, \"D3S4\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d02\", \"start\": 374, \"end\": 467, \"text\": \"Banknotes of 1 , 2 , were later introduced in 1942 , and 5,000 banknotes were added in 1943 .\"}, \"D3S5\": {\"document_number\": 3, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d02\", \"start\": 468, \"end\": 496, \"text\": \"[ 10 ] The Kuna started with\"}, \"D4S1\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d03\", \"start\": 0, \"end\": 64, \"text\": \"introduced in the Independent State of Croatia on 26 July 1941 .\"}, \"D4S2\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d03\", \"start\": 65, \"end\": 205, \"text\": \"Italian lira and German Reichsmark were printed parallel to the kuna and in occupied countries , but were not legal tender in Nazi Germany .\"}, \"D4S3\": {\"document_number\": 4, \"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d03\", \"start\": 206, \"end\": 490, \"text\": \"[ 4 ] The kuna replaced the Yugoslav dinar at par and was fixed to the Reichsmark with dual exchange rates , [ citation needed ] one fixed at 20 kuna = 1 RM , the other a state-stimulated rate of : 31 December 1941 - 25.00 Kn = 1 ℛℳ 31 December 1942 - 37.50 Kn = 1 ℛℳ 31 December 1943\"}}, \"original_cards\": [{\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d00:c0\", \"entity\": \"Croatian money\", \"scope\": \"introduction date\", \"claim\": \"The modern kuna was introduced on May 30, 1994.\", \"answer\": \"May 30, 1994\", \"quote\": \"The modern kuna was introduced on May 30, 1994, starting a transitional period from the Croatian dinar, ending on December 31, 1994.\", \"original_document_number\": 1}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d01:c0\", \"entity\": \"Croatian money\", \"scope\": \"introduction date\", \"claim\": \"The Croatian kuna was introduced in the Independent State of Croatia on 26 July 1941.\", \"answer\": \"26 July 1941\", \"quote\": \"The Croatian kuna was introduced in the Independent State of Croatia (NDH) on 26 July 1941.\", \"original_document_number\": 2}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d02\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d02:c0\", \"entity\": \"Croatian money\", \"scope\": \"first introduction\", \"claim\": \"The Croatian money (Kuna) was first introduced on 26 July 1941 by the Independent State of Croatia during World War II.\", \"answer\": \"26 July 1941\", \"quote\": \"[ 9 ] A 5000 Independent State of Croatia kuna banknote , 1943 series From 1941–1945 during World War II in Yugoslavia , the Independent State of Croatia officially adopted the Kuna as the currency for the first time on 26 July 1941 .\", \"original_document_number\": 3}, {\"doc_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d03\", \"card_id\": \"ramdocs-0122-70ee675e9688:d03:c0\", \"entity\": \"Croatian money\", \"scope\": \"introduction date\", \"claim\": \"The Croatian money was introduced on 26 July 1941.\", \"answer\": \"26 July 1941\", \"quote\": \"introduced in the Independent State of Croatia on 26 July 1941 .\", \"original_document_number\": 4}], \"R5_answers\": [\"26 July 1941\", \"May 30, 1994\"], \"candidates\": [{\"id\": \"C0\", \"answer\": \"26 July 1941\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}, {\"id\": \"C1\", \"answer\": \"May 30, 1994\", \"origin\": \"R5\"}]}"
